      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "A soft X-ray device for intravascular radiation therapy of restenosis is characterized in terms of dose delivery for several artery configurations, including arteries with implanted stents, calcified plaque, and noncentered sources."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND MATERIALS",
          "content": "The Monte Carlo code MCNP4B was used to determine the X-ray fluence and energy spectra for 15, 20, and 30-kV X-ray source generating voltages. Dose as a function of distance was calculated under a variety of artery conditions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Calculated depth-dose profiles for the X-ray sources are within presumed artery dose tolerance limits for the range of generating voltages considered. Treatment times to deliver 8 Gy to the adventitia range from 2.7 minutes to 6.7 minutes for the 20-kV generating voltage and a 3-cm-long lesion, depending on the diameter of the artery. The does perturbation due to stent wires or calcified plaque is found to be more severe for the X-ray sources than for the radioactive sources. The effects of noncentering are found to be similar for radioactive sources and X-ray sources with generating voltages of 20 kV or higher."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The results of this study indicate that soft X-ray sources are suitable candidates for intravascular radiation therapy over a wide range of artery sizes, tissue compositions, and stent configurations."
        }
      ]},
